The current voice market has moved from basic dictation to AI-assisted speech: products such as Wispr Flow, Superwhisper and TalkTastic already let users speak naturally, clean up punctuation and structure, and use some application context to produce better writing. Users clearly value the reduced friction of speaking at the speed of thought, while the newer products also show growing demand for personalization through styles, modes and vocabulary. However, most of the market still treats voice primarily as a way to enter or improve text: TalkTastic describes itself as a voice keyboard for apps where you can type or paste, while Superwhisper's modes are largely built around how spoken input should be processed. As these products become more intelligent, a new problem also appears—how much should software interpret versus simply preserve what the user meant? Users continue to notice unwanted rewriting, formatting, reliability and privacy trade-offs, while multilingual and code-mixed speech is still less central to the overall interaction model. This leaves a broader opportunity for voice to become not just another way of typing, but a more natural way of interacting with what is already on the screen, across different applications, languages and contexts. 
It has taken us many years to learn how computers lead us to work.
We are given instructions about where to click, which buttons to push, which shortcuts we are to remember and how to phrase our statements so that the software will understand them.
The same approach has generally been used in the case of voice-you speak something, turn it into text, and then proceed.
Kivi should change that relationship.
Kivi ought to appear as a voice function that is always accessible, not as yet another application that we have to open.
Kivi should be able to accompany me each time I use Chrome, Gmail, Docs, Cursor, ChatGPT or any other service.
I can integrate Kivi Anywhere into any project that I am currently working on, and Kivi Mic makes my current microphone smarter without forcing me to change to a new workflow(Similar to Gboard) .
The app mentioned below should stay where it is currently located.Kivi appears, provides his assistance and then gets out of the way.
The interaction should be simple enough to explain in one line:
Begin by stating the point, then discuss it, go on to understand it, then look forward to previewing it, and end by applying it.
I ought to be able to choose an item that is important on the screen and then notice it turn into Kivi green.
The fact that this selection has been made is exactly what Kivi understands I mean.
By that stage I can just state what I want, such as "shorten this", "explain this", "put this into bullet points" or "make this sound less formal".
The screen has Kivi listed as the location.
This is what my voice shows Kivi.
Kivi works out what the meaning is.
Kivi would be useful when working with information.
It allows me to turn a stream of thoughts into a structured format.
This enables me to write and edit both text and code without having to pause and work out an exact prompt.
It enables me to understand papers, articles, documents and code and has the capability of explaining things out loud through TTS as I am reading.
Using Communicate, I can take one idea and put it across in a different manner depending on who I am addressing.
The system keeps the items that I don't want to lose and helps Kivi to learn what is important to me.
These can be seen through five simple ways in which Kivi can help me: Think, Write, Understand, Communicate and Remember. Think helps me turn a messy stream of thoughts into decisions, questions, tasks or structure. Write helps me create and edit both text and code. Understand helps me make sense of things that I am reading or looking at. Communicate helps me express the same thought differently depending on the person, language or situation wheter in voice or text . Remember helps me keep things I might otherwise forget and carry useful things forward for later and also it remembers my styles.
Language should work in that way.
I never should have had to stop and decide if I am now speaking Tamil or if I am now speaking English.
It is natural for people to use a mix of languages, and this should be the case in both India and in Kivi.
STT ought to reflect the way people really speak, and Kivi should be able to give the result in the language and form that is suitable for the situation.
Kivi Mic could take it a step further in this regard.
Because of the speech-to-speech (STS) technology which Sarvams has developed, language no longer has to be a barrier when you are speaking to someone else.
I know how to speak Tamil and am capable of sending a voice message in Hindi or having a live conversation with someone while they listen to the conversation in their own language.
All we desire is that people should not convert what they are thinking into words before speaking.
The plan is that they should talk normally while Kivi handles the translation.
The main feature of what lies ahead for Kivis is personalization.
Kivi shouldn't be exactly like everybody else.
I should be able to teach Kivi the words that it spells incorrectly, make up my own shortcuts, build a new mode based on just a few examples, keep the way that I want things to be written, and fix Kivi when it gets a word wrong.
The more time passes, the more Kivi will come to know not my voice but rather my vocabulary, my habits and the shortcuts that I use when expressing what I want.( Personalization Moat)
That is the reason why it might be difficult to leave Kivi.
There could be one product or another that offers speech-to-text, text-to-speech, translation or rewriting.
It won't be able to instantly replicate my dictionary, my shortcuts, my styles, my modes and all the things that Kivi has picked up from me.
Kivi should still know its limits.
It must show me what it considers to be my question in cases where it is uncertain and enable me to decide before any changes are made- Human in the Loop (HITL)
The end goal is simple:
I should be able to indicate clearly what i want using our natural state of speech and have my computer understand my meaning and the context.


